==Latin==

===Etymology===
From {{term|dies|diēs|day|lang=la}} and {{term|Mercurii|lang=la}}, genitive of {{term|Mercurius||Mercury|lang=la}}.
Late Latin (late 2nd or 3rd century) calque of Koine Greek ἡμέρα Ἕρμου ''heméra Hérmou''. The association of the seven week days with the seven classical planets is first attested in the  ''Anthologiarum'' by  [[:w:Vettius Valens|Vettius Valens]], ca. AD 170.

===Noun===
'''[[dies Mercurii]]'''

